WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:01.830
Narrator: A city in China wants to launch

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:05.690
an artificial moon into
orbit by 2020 as a way

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.750
to reduce energy needs
by replacing street light

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.100
with artificial moonlight.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.410
Regardless of whether or not they succeed,

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.820
their ambitious idea
really made us wonder.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.863
What would happen if Earth had two moons?

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.830
Well, it wouldn't be pretty.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.270
Imagine the moon's identical
twin comes hurtling by

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:31.040
and is trapped by Earth's gravity.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.030
As it settles into orbit
halfway between Earth

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:38.350
and our original moon, it
yanks violently at the oceans.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:41.110
In the real world, this
is how our original moon

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.200
helps generate tides.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.400
So, the second moon
would amplify the effect

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.520
causing peak tides that
would be six times higher,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.850
eroding shorelines and flooding many

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.970
of our world's greatest
cities including New York,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.643
Singapore, and London, gone.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.520
But not all destruction
would happen on Earth.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:06.700
The combined pull of the
planet and the original moon

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.370
would also yank on the second moon.

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.700
The second moon would be
caught in a tug of war

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.160
between earth and the original moon.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.310
The gravitational pull back
and forth from both ends

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.620
would warp the second moon's surface

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:23.220
triggering tremendous
volcanic activity flooding

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.500
the second moon's surface
with red hot rivers of lava

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.440
just like hundreds of the
volcanoes you see today

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.683
on Jupiter's hellish moon, Io.

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:35.630
But even that's not the
end of the spectacle.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.730
Right now, our current moon
is spiraling away from Earth

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.600
at 3.8 centimeters a year.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.400
That's about how fast
your fingernails grow.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.100
At the same time, it pulls on the Earth

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.720
slowing down the planet's
rotation which is actually

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.810
lengthening our days by around one second

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.180
every 40, 000 years.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.750
It may not sound like much,
but with two moons in place,

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:04.050
it would accelerate
this process even more.

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:08.740
Millions of years from now,
the day will have grown by 16%,

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:11.930
lasting longer than 28 hours.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.100
Now a little extra time in
the day may sound pretty nice,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.760
but here's the problem,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.360
the extra moon would drift
towards the current moon

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.600
and that's where the real danger comes in.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.930
After millions of years,
the two moons would collide.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:31.080
The impact would be so massive,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.400
it would rip the very
core of the moons apart.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.480
Lava would erupt from their center

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.820
like a runny egg in space
casting a vivid, red light

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.900
in the sky on Earth.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.840
Meanwhile, debris would go
hurtling in all directions

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.550
where some of it would
inevitably strike Earth

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.800
forming massive craters miles wide.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.570
It would be an apocalypse
for all life on Earth.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.210
And what didn't hit the planet
would instead be trapped

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.950
by Earth's gravity
forming a ring of debris

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.171
around the equator similar
to the rings around Saturn.

00:03:05.171 --> 00:03:07.160
But not for long.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.360
Within just a few years, those
chunks would clump together

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.360
forming one, large, single body.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.150
Perhaps any life that survived
will call it the moon,

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.563
or maybe something even better.

